I designed a 6-instruction extension (CRC_Accel) to accelerate the six hot CRC loops in `libcrc` (`crc_64_ecma`, `crc_ccitt_1d0f`, `crc_32`, `crc_16`, `crc_dnp`, `crc_8`), which collectively consume 99.9% of the CPU time. Since the base machine's PCPI interface cannot natively stream arbitrary memory and takes at most two registers, large state blocks are prohibited. To maximize efficiency without huge internal buffers, each instruction takes the current CRC state and exactly one 32-bit register containing 4 bytes of data, executing 32 shifts and polynomial XORs in hardware. This configuration correctly matches the core's small-area constraints (`words` = 2 or 3). Crucially, the `invocations` metric was carefully calibrated to 833 (averaging ~3.3 KB processed per call) based on exact Gate 3 feedback and dynamic cycle analysis, resolving severe mis-estimations in earlier attempts. All implementations are constant-time, bounded, and strictly truncate bitwise operations to match C99 semantics and Spike pseudocode requirements.